<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Latium</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Latium was the region of central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tiber-river">Tiber</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bay-of-naples">Bay of Naples</a>, inhabited by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latins</a>, who gave their name to the Latin language. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> rose to dominate the Latin League and absorbed the region after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin-war">Latin War</a> of 340–338 BCE. Its name survives in the modern Italian region of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lazio">Lazio</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/latium.jpg" alt="Latium" width="320" height="227" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: William Robert Shepherd · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Latium_et_Campania.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Latium was originally a small triangle of fertile volcanic soil, called Old Latium, on the left bank of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tiber-river">Tiber</a>, extending north to the River Anio and south-east to the Pontine Marshes and the Circeian promontory. The right bank was held by the Etruscan city of Veii. The earliest Latium was the country of the Latini, whose recognised centre was the dormant volcano of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/monte-cavo">Mons Albanus</a>, the Alban Mount, 20 kilometres south-east of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>. On its second-highest peak, Monte Cavo, stood a temple to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jupiter-latiaris">Jupiter Latiaris</a> where the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latins</a> held state functions. The name is most likely derived from the Latin latus, wide, expressing the idea of flat land in contrast to the Sabine high country.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The region had settled agricultural populations from the early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bronze-age">Bronze Age</a>. By the 10th century BCE archaeology records numerous villages whose people grew spelt, barley, grapes, olives, apples and figs. Etruscan cultural and political influence grew from about the 8th century BCE, but the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/etruscans">Etruscans</a> could not assert hegemony over small, autonomous <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> city-states. Alba Longa, on the Alban range, was regarded as the primitive seat of the Latin stock and the mother city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>. Tusculum, Tibur, Praeneste, Gabii, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lavinium">Lavinium</a> and Laurentum were other ancient Latin centres.</p><p>The Latin League tied the communities together by religion and descent. Its ritual was the Latin festival, at which an ox was sacrificed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jupiter-latiaris">Jupiter Latiaris</a> on the Alban Mount, and thirty villages were originally entitled to take part. Alba Longa held religious primacy but never political power. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> soon acquired the presidency, and Alba Longa was destroyed in the mid-7th century BCE. According to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/livy">Livy</a> it was razed, except for its temples, by King Tullus. Rome's generous grant of citizenship to outsiders, even former slaves, gave it more manpower than any other Latin city. Rome later defeated Veii and the Italic neighbours, and the name Latium came to cover the area south of Rome too, as Latium adiectum, as far as Casinum.</p><p>Augustus made Latium, with Campagna, Region I of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>. After the Gothic War of 535–554 the Roman Duchy came to the Byzantine emperor, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> later took over the region. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/papacy">papacy</a> returned control of Latium in 1353–1367 after the period at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/avignon">Avignon</a>, and from the middle of the 16th century it was administered as part of the Papal States. It became part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-italy">Kingdom of Italy</a> after the capture of Rome on 20 September 1870.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The modern Italian region of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lazio">Lazio</a>, called Latium in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a>, is somewhat larger than ancient Latium, including a large area of ancient southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/etruria">Etruria</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sabina">Sabina</a>. It contains the national capital, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, and holds many works of art and architecture. It gained a significant level of autonomy after a constitutional reform in 2001.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Latium” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After Carthage's defeat by the Syracusan coalition in 480 BCE and the Battle of Cumae in 474 BCE, <b>Latium</b> and Campania passed to Romans and Samnites.</blockquote><a href="/empire/etruscan-league">Etruscan League · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>The new state was a city of perhaps 25,000 to 40,000 people on the Tiber in <b>Latium</b>, already one of the largest in central Italy.</blockquote><a href="/empire/roman-republic">Roman Republic · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>It was the language of the Latini, the people of <b>Latium</b>, the plain south of the Tiber, and of their most successful city, Rome.</blockquote><a href="/language/latin">Latin · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>Rome and <b>Latium</b> were annexed after a plebiscite on 2 October.</blockquote><a href="/movement/unification-of-italy">Unification of Italy · Outcomes</a></li><li><blockquote>In the second half Aeneas settles in <b>Latium</b>, where King Latinus is told by oracles to give his daughter Lavinia to a stranger rather than to Turnus of the Rutuli.</blockquote><a href="/work/aeneid">Aeneid · What It Is</a></li><li><blockquote>The Aequi lived in the upper valleys of the Aniene, Turano and Salto rivers in the Apennines east of <b>Latium</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/aequi">Aequi</a></li></ul></section>
